# The Duty of Those with Strength of Heart
Tamil title: உள்ள உரம் படைத்தோர் கடமை

The lamp of knowledge he lit guides our lives. That man of wisdom gave us a bell-lamp that drives away mental darkness. His spirit of inquiry, intellect, desire to reform people and tireless struggle against caste, social division and superstition astonished observers. Many mocked him, but his heart did not break; many opposed him, but he would not submit; trouble came, but he did not wilt. New ideas entered people's minds slowly, yet he did not tire. With deep faith that his principles must ultimately triumph, this fearless warrior of moral struggle opposed entrenched social systems until the end and rejoiced as they began to break.

Facing the priestly “three-town” order glaring at him, he mocked: “Why do these people unseen in any other town not use their magical power to curse me into a cow or tree? If they can, let them curse this sinner's head to shatter into a thousand pieces!”

The orthodox raged that his destruction was near. He laughed, saying they abused him because they could do nothing. To cheats, religious fanatics and priests he first appeared an exclamation mark, then a question mark, and finally a danger signal. Conservatives predicted his noise would end and his ideas burn out. Instead, as opposition strengthened, his campaign grew still stronger and wider. Ordinary people first feared he might be sinful, then began ridiculing the priests.

A priest might ask Paandu what arrangements he had made for his father's death anniversary, assuming the common man remained unenlightened. Paandu would reply, “I have received no message that my father was satisfied with what I have already done. Why continue the rite?” The almanac bearer would mutter that the age had decayed and the sinner had forgotten religion under the old man's influence. The commoner would laugh, “There goes the postbox to the next world!”

A timid person might warn him not to insult the “great caste.” He would answer: “What harm comes from exposing that trickster? Will my tongue rot? What has this great caste done to the elder who daily exposes their worth, makes their four Vedas stink, burns Puranic lies and pulverises the epics? What did their mantras accomplish?” Even the coward would gain new courage.

Older people began fearing widows' tears. This fearless reformer proclaimed that a widow possessed life and the right to remarry, and that only the dull-witted and selfish forgot it. Women wiped their tears and began attending to the idea; eyes once shedding grief began firing questions. Priests had known only grieving widows, not questioning ones.

He exposed corruption in Hindu religion without fear or indulgence, condemned the habit of treating Brahmins as earthly gods, attacked customs long called sacred instruments of happiness here and hereafter, explained their ruinous effects and spread rationalism. Hindu and Brahmin leaders condemned him; national leaders mocked the work as useless. He did not tire. He struck again and again until antiquity lost strength.

His support was a small army of young people; his opponents were millionaires, decorated scholars, trustees and heads of monasteries. They threatened to burn his house and make him a corpse before dawn. He did not fear, and the army of reason beside him grew. He criticised the pundit's conduct, the commoner's blindness, the rich person's counterfeit devotion and the religious advocate's mental darkness in speech and writing. Victory came; a fortress of warriors formed; the morning star rose.

On one side sounded “God will protect us,” with sectarian marks, sacred ash and dictionaries; on another stood wearers of Gandhi caps. Hymns on one side and rage on the other, with this hero and his rational army between them. The patriot said the country must gain freedom; religion said chant the Lord's name; this fighter said, “Let us think.”

They called it empty talk, sophistry, treason, religious betrayal and great sin. What of today? Premier Omandurar, Minister Kala Venkata Rao, scholar C. R. Reddy, Thiru. Vi. Kalyanasundaranar and Rajamannar praise this reformer's rational capacity, wealth of thought, zeal, revolutionary mind and progressive programme. Newspapers compete to honour him; *The Hindu* writes an editorial and radio broadcasts his fame. They celebrate that he abolished caste distinction, subdued religious fraud, gave widows a new life, broke superstition and elevated knowledge.

One dignitary says that while everyone concentrated upon political freedom and forgot society, this hero alone placed social reform at the front. Others credit his rare labour for the decline of caste frenzy and religious deceit, the spread of intermarriage and widow remarriage, and a new social and intellectual awakening. People of all parties and none gather to salute his fearlessness and say he opened a new epoch. Those who once abused and resisted him now sincerely praise his service and bow before him.

Our comrades may incredulously ask whether Omandurar, Rajamannar, Kala Venkata Rao and *The Hindu* have truly acquired the virtue of praising the commander of rationalism. Their disbelief is reasonable, and our report is also true. These dignitaries did praise a fearless social reformer, but not Periyar Ramasamy, who now performs the same work before their eyes despite repression and prison. If that generosity had arisen toward him, Tamil country would begin to flourish.

They praised the late Andhra reformer Kandukuri Veeresalingam.

Veeresalingam was wise and laboured against social corruption amid orthodox opposition. Through intellect he produced an Andhra renaissance and cleansed minds; his criticism, satire and research were bombs in the citadel of orthodoxy. His courage deserves admiration. We rejoice that the Premier and others praised him and join that praise. But honesty requires an answer: if they honour him for that work, why forget the person doing it more explicitly today amid their own colleagues' hostility?

They did not praise Veeresalingam for wealth, office, dress, priesthood or monastic status, but because he was a reformer and rationalist who fought a society fragmented by caste and religion, with an arrogant few on one side and a bewildered multitude on the other. Is it just to forget the person pursuing that same intellectual revolution before them? Others may call such forgetting statecraft or possess hardened hearts. Omandurar's heart is said to be a spring of noble principles. Why then does he honour the Andhra reformer but threaten the Tamil reformer? If he can appreciate the fragrance of the jasmine, does the flower of Tamil country have no fragrance?

You rejoice that Veeresalingam condemned caste, while Periyar is abused for doing so. You praise Veeresalingam for exposing Hindu corruption, yet remain silent while Periyar is reviled as atheist for identical work. You marvel that Veeresalingam promoted inter-caste, widow and love marriages, but lose that generosity toward the Self-Respect leader who made reform marriages without meaningless rites or Aryan priesthood commonplace in Tamil country.

Here lies the explanation for Tamil Nadu's degradation. Leaders can praise distant or dead figures as noble scholars and heroes and speak of ending caste, religious deception and superstition. Yet their minds refuse to praise the living person who made those principles his life's purpose. We worry particularly whether Omandurar, reputedly devoted to morality, truth, honesty and justice, will become merely one of that crowd.

If their praise of Veeresalingam came from the heart, they cannot fail to honour Periyar. Otherwise either selfish deceit prevents them, or their praise was empty display. Imagine someone professing love for music but saying he does not know whether Isai Arasu Dandapani Desikar can sing; someone seeing a ship at sea but pretending not to while singing of an old voyage; or a scholar claiming to admire Indian architecture but saying the Taj Mahal is perhaps in Iran. Could such a lecture continue?

What category fits those who profess admiration for Veeresalingam's needed reform principles while abusing Periyar for performing that work today? Shall hands placing flowers upon the departed reformer throw stones at the leader who has travelled farther on his road? Even if others refuse thought, the Premier should not. We say so not because of his office but because of the honesty regarded as the foremost Tamil virtue. His future actions will show whether our confidence is right.

Periyar's aim is to end the calamity of caste. Honest Congress members do not deny that, and courageous ones now openly condemn caste. He has given them an opportunity through a statement asking that the new Constitution remove arrangements and labels marking caste distinction and create the foundation for a casteless society. What can admirers of Veeresalingam answer?

They failed thus far to support Periyar's present struggle; let that pass. If their praise came from the heart, should they not now discuss and support his statement? Sankaracharyas, monastery heads and orthodox ritualists will not want a casteless society, for it leaves them no privileged place. The statement is not addressed to them but appeals to receptive Congress members, an appeal that may become a warning if ignored.

Omandurar and those like him cannot dismiss it as somebody's irrelevant statement. Periyar is not asking for a few posts as reward for building a party. His document is not a petition but a challenge: you praise Veeresalingam and say Gandhi laboured for a casteless society; now show how far your action follows your words. Support it, communicate your position firmly to the Congress high command, and let honest journals endorse it.

Thus far only the weekly *Tamilmani* has shown this honesty. It writes:

“The idea of building a society without caste distinction has recently spread rapidly. Everyone agrees caste differences should not exist; no journal fails to condemn them and no speaker avoids the subject. Even thoroughgoing orthodox people quote the *Bhagavad Gita* and preach harmony. But all this has remained speech and print, with not the slightest practical change.

“The reason is government's failure to attend to abolition. If we truly want equality, law is indispensable. The recent statement by Dravidar Kazhagam leader E. V. Ramasamy can greatly assist. He advises the central government that the new Constitution must not retain distinctions such as Brahmin, Sudra and Harijan, and that words denoting caste divisions must not appear in any political, religious or social authority. We welcome and support his desire to remove the conditions that mix caste division into life.

“E.V.R. makes another important point: it is cruel to abolish concessions based upon caste without first legally removing the words and distinctions themselves. If communal representation is abolished merely in the name of ending caste while caste remains, backward societies lose their path to progress.

“The central government must address the foundation and create a condition in which no authority can determine a person's caste. Only then can a casteless society truly arise; otherwise it will remain old liquor in a new pot.”

What will other journals and leaders do? If they praised Veeresalingam from genuine belief in his principles, they must support Periyar's statement and work to implement it. Otherwise any intelligent person will conclude they praised him not because he was a reformer, rationalist and architect of a new world, but because he was Veeresalingam Pantulu, a Brahmin.

“Without strength in the heart,
Without the power of honesty,
They speak deceit, little bird,
Heroes only in words!”

---NOTES---
The Tamil OCR source is retained verbatim. No publication date is visible. The opening initially withholds the reformer's name as a rhetorical device; the translation preserves that reveal. “உள்ள உரம்” is rendered “strength of heart,” following the closing poetic quotation. Historical caste labels and the article's sharp religious and political comparisons are preserved as source rhetoric, not endorsement. The *Tamilmani* passage is translated in full from OCR and should be checked against the printed weekly before scholarly quotation. The phrase “வீரேசலிங்கம் பந்துலு (பார்ப்பனர்)” is retained in meaning as the author's closing accusation; the parenthetical caste identification may itself require verification against the scan and historical record.
